  ],
  "opening": "In Chapter 6, we saw that applying a reversible circuit \\(U_f\\) to a superposition of inputs evaluates \\(f(x)\\) on every input at once, yet measuring the register reveals only one random pair \\((x, f(x))\\). Moreover, because the target qubit retains \\(f(x)\\), branches with different outputs cannot interfere.",
  "opening_continuation": "We first introduce a model in which a function is accessed through queries and pose a problem that asks for a relation between function values. We then show how to record function values in the phases of amplitudes rather than in the target register, leading to our first quantum algorithm.",
  "intro_continuation": "Classically, finding out whether \\(f(0) = f(1)\\) requires evaluating \\(f\\) twice—once on \\(0\\) and once on \\(1\\)—because knowing \\(f(0)\\) alone leaves both possibilities open. Can a quantum computation answer this question using only a single evaluation of \\(U_f\\)?"
}
